Q: How to transform all text links in a JS string into HTML links? A: <p>I know of three different approaches you can take, I've used all of these.</p>
<h2>Regex (no dependencies)</h2>
<pre><code>const myText = &quot;To get more help please click on our FAQ link at https://www.faqlink.com or talk to an agent now at https://www.agentlink.com&quot;;

const result = myText.replace(
  /(https?:\/\/[^\s]+)/g,
  '&lt;a target=&quot;_blank&quot; rel=&quot;noopener noreferrer&quot; href=&quot;$1&quot;&gt;$1&lt;/a&gt;'
);

console.log(result);
</code></pre>
<p>Essentially, with this one, you just wrap it in an <code>&lt;a&gt;</code></p>
<h2>Regex (but markdown style)</h2>
<pre><code>const myText = &quot;To get more help please click on our [https://www.faqlink.com](FAQ link) or talk to an agent now [https://www.agentlink.com](here)&quot;;

export const markdownLinks = (str) =&gt; str.replace(
  /\[([^\]]+)\]\((https?:\/\/)?([^)]+)\)/g,
  (_, label, protocol, url) =&gt; {
    const href = protocol ? protocol + url : &quot;https://&quot; + url;
    return `&lt;a target=&quot;_blank&quot; rel=&quot;noopener noreferrer&quot; href=&quot;${href}&quot;&gt;${label}&lt;/a&gt;`;
  }
);

console.log(markdownLinks(myText));
</code></pre>
<p>I work with markdown a lot so this one is pretty useful, easy to slap into a utility file and call it a day.</p>
<h2><a href="https://github.com/gregjacobs/Autolinker.js/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Autolinker</a> (dependency)</h2>
<p>I use this in my project because it made things a little cleaner, as it's expandable.</p>
<pre><code>import Autolinker from &quot;autolinker&quot;;

const myText = &quot;To get more help please click on our FAQ link at https://www.faqlink.com or talk to an agent now at https://www.agentlink.com&quot;;

const result = Autolinker.link(myText, {
  newWindow: true
});

console.log(result);
</code></pre>
<p><strong>Quick Note:</strong> if performance is a bottleneck (which isn't really the case in most webapps), you'll need to use something other than the regex engine, as it can get costly. Probably something along the lines of while loops and recreations of the string. But, it doesn't sound like your use-case because you're already using <code>.replace</code>, so I think you're all good to use the above.</p>
